{:edb/committed true :edb/basis-t 2785 :edb/replayed false :edb/tx-hash "06f3d926b5eadb9950e8228a040edf80af0e80568d71f1d90dcb01eb3d32bf3e" :edb/db-before-t 2784 :edb/db-after-t 2785 :edb/tx-data [[13194139536097 50 #inst "2026-10-09T14:57:06.215Z" 13194139536097 true] [17592186164189 1104 #edb/ref 17592186164191 13194139536097 true] [17592186164192 1104 #edb/ref 17592186164193 13194139536097 true] [17592186164195 1104 #edb/ref 17592186164198 13194139536097 true] [17592186164443 1104 #edb/ref 17592186164444 13194139536097 true] [17592186164446 1104 #edb/ref 17592186164449 13194139536097 true] [17592186164450 1104 #edb/ref 17592186164452 13194139536097 true] [17592186166845 1020 #edb/ref 17592186383383 13194139536097 true] [17592186166845 1020 #edb/ref 17592186383390 13194139536097 true] [17592186166846 1020 #edb/ref 17592186383397 13194139536097 true] [17592186167138 1091 #edb/ref 17592186045463 13194139536097 true] [17592186167138 1100 "$$\n{\\begin{aligned}{x}^{′}(t)=-2x(t) \\\\ {y}^{′}(t)=3y(t)\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nSelect the correct options below regarding the given decoupled system of linear differential equations.$\\,$\nThe $x$-axis is a {{field-1}} line.\n\n$\\,$\nThe $y$-axis is a {{field-2}} line.\n\n$\\,$\nThe equilibrium point (origin) is {{field-3}}." 13194139536097 true] [17592186167138 1100 "${x^{′}(t) =-2x(t); y^{′}(t) = 3y(t)$\nSelect the correct options below regarding the given decoupled system of linear differential equations.\nThe $x$-axis is a {{field-1}} line.\n\nThe $y$-axis is a {{field-2}} line.\n\nThe equilibrium point (origin) is {{field-3}}." 13194139536097 false] [17592186167138 1145 "Writing our system in matrix form, we have\n\n$$\n{x}^{′}(t)=[\\begin{aligned}-2 & 0 \\\\ 0 & 3\\end{aligned}]x(t)\n$$\n\nwhere $x(t)=[\\begin{aligned}x(t) \\\\ y(t)\\end{aligned}].$\n\nRecall that a homogeneous system of linear differential equations has a single equilibrium (fixed point) at the origin.\n\nSince our system is decoupled, its eigenvectors are the following:\n\n- Eigenvalue ${\\lambda }_{1}=2$ with the eigenvector $[\\begin{aligned}1 \\\\ 0\\end{aligned}].$ The corresponding straight-line solutions of the form\n${c}_{1}{e}^{-2t}[\\begin{aligned}1 \\\\ 0\\end{aligned}]$\nlie on the $x$-axis and approach the origin as $t\\to \\infty .$ So, the $x$-axis is the $\\text{stable}$ line.\n\n- Eigenvalue ${\\lambda }_{2}=3$ with the eigenvector $[\\begin{aligned}0 \\\\ 1\\end{aligned}].$ The corresponding straight-line solutions of the form\n${c}_{2}{e}^{3t}[\\begin{aligned}0 \\\\ 1\\end{aligned}]$\nlie on the $y$-axis and approach infinity as $t\\to \\infty .$ So, the $y$-axis is the $\\text{unstable}$ line.\n\nTherefore, the equilibrium point (origin) is $\\text{a saddle point}.$ \n\n**Note:** The phase portrait of the system looks as follows:\n\n![](/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/differential/14076662/assets/0948dac4f8a192018baee1339d7334d573592d7b7bf4383c1297cc2431e51201.png)" 13194139536097 true] [17592186167174 1091 #edb/ref 17592186045463 13194139536097 true] [17592186167174 1100 "$$\n{\\begin{aligned}{x}^{′}(t)=x(t) \\\\ {y}^{′}(t)=2y(t)\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nSelect the correct options below regarding the given decoupled system of linear differential equations.$\\,$\nThe $x$-axis is a {{field-1}} line.\n\n$\\,$\nThe $y$-axis is a {{field-2}} line.\n\n$\\,$\nThe equilibrium point (origin) is {{field-3}}." 13194139536097 true] [17592186167174 1100 "${x^{′}(t) = x(t); y^{′}(t) = 2y(t)$\nSelect the correct options below regarding the given decoupled system of linear differential equations.\nThe $x$-axis is a {{field-1}} line.\n\nThe $y$-axis is a {{field-2}} line.\n\nThe equilibrium point (origin) is {{field-3}}." 13194139536097 false] [17592186167174 1145 "Writing our system in matrix form, we have\n\n$$\n{x}^{′}(t)=[\\begin{aligned}1 & 0 \\\\ 0 & 2\\end{aligned}]x(t)\n$$\n\nwhere $x(t)=[\\begin{aligned}x(t) \\\\ y(t)\\end{aligned}].$\n\nRecall that a homogeneous system of linear differential equations has a single equilibrium (fixed point) at the origin.\n\nSince our system is decoupled, its eigenvectors are the following:\n\n- Eigenvalue ${\\lambda }_{1}=1$ with the eigenvector $[\\begin{aligned}1 \\\\ 0\\end{aligned}].$ The corresponding straight-line solutions of the form\n${c}_{1}{e}^{t}[\\begin{aligned}1 \\\\ 0\\end{aligned}]$\nlie on the $x$-axis and move away from the origin as $t\\to \\infty .$ So, the $x$-axis is the $\\text{unstable}$ line.\n\n- Eigenvalue ${\\lambda }_{2}=2$ with the eigenvector $[\\begin{aligned}0 \\\\ 1\\end{aligned}].$ The corresponding straight-line solutions of the form\n${c}_{2}{e}^{2t}[\\begin{aligned}0 \\\\ 1\\end{aligned}]$\nlie on the $y$-axis and also move away from the origin as $t\\to \\infty .$ So, the $y$-axis is the $\\text{unstable}$ line.\n\nTherefore, the equilibrium point (origin) is $\\text{an unstable node (source)}.$ \n\n**Note:** The phase portrait of the system looks as follows:\n\n![](/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/differential/14076662/assets/19cd34ffc84e4a904d87cdfe0d8b6721861a0a39025484946dbe471a5ea4ad87.png)" 13194139536097 true] [17592186383383 1017 #edb/ref 17592186383384 13194139536097 true] [17592186383383 1089 "q-332705" 13194139536097 true] [17592186383383 1091 #edb/ref 17592186045463 13194139536097 true] [17592186383383 1100 "![](/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/differential/14076662/assets/98ffa8fec669ce09d22b5b904b710632314f7b22e0eb5098719c41147c186975.png)\n\nConsider the phase portrait for a decoupled system above.\nWhich of the following systems could correspond to the given portrait?" 13194139536097 true] [17592186383383 1113 #uuid "1ced5b6b-97cb-5a57-b3b4-0d4662b19d0d" 13194139536097 true] [17592186383383 1145 "Writing a decoupled system in matrix form, we have\n\n$$\n{x}^{′}(t)=[\\begin{aligned}{\\lambda }_{1} & 0 \\\\ 0 & {\\lambda }_{2}\\end{aligned}]x(t)\n$$\n\nwhere $x(t)=[\\begin{aligned}x(t) \\\\ y(t)\\end{aligned}].$\n\nSince our system is decoupled, its eigenvectors are parallel to the axes. These correspond to so-called straight-line solutions of the form\n\n$$\n{c}_{1}{e}^{{\\lambda }_{1}t}[\\begin{aligned}1 \\\\ 0\\end{aligned}]\\,\\text{and}\\,{c}_{2}{e}^{{\\lambda }_{2}t}[\\begin{aligned}0 \\\\ 1\\end{aligned}].\n$$\n\nNow, notice that:\n\n- The tangents along the $x$-axis are pointing away from the origin, meaning that the solutions of the form \n${c}_{1}{e}^{{\\lambda }_{1}t}[\\begin{aligned}1 \\\\ 0\\end{aligned}]$\nmust approach infinity as $t\\to \\infty .$ So, we must have ${\\lambda }_{1}>0.$\n\n- The tangents along the $y$-axis are also pointing away from the origin, meaning that the solutions of the form \n${c}_{2}{e}^{{\\lambda }_{2}t}[\\begin{aligned}0 \\\\ 1\\end{aligned}]$\nmust also approach infinity as $t\\to \\infty .$ So, we must have ${\\lambda }_{2}>0.$\n\nAmong the options, the only system that satisfies ${\\lambda }_{1}>0$ and ${\\lambda }_{2}>0$ is the following:\n\n$$\n{\\begin{aligned}{x}^{′}(t)=2x(t) \\\\ {y}^{′}(t)=5y(t)\\end{aligned}\n$$" 13194139536097 true] [17592186383384 1000 #edb/ref 17592186383385 13194139536097 true] [17592186383384 1000 #edb/ref 17592186383386 13194139536097 true] [17592186383384 1000 #edb/ref 17592186383387 13194139536097 true] [17592186383384 1000 #edb/ref 17592186383388 13194139536097 true] [17592186383384 1000 #edb/ref 17592186383389 13194139536097 true] [17592186383384 1040 #edb/ref 17592186045450 13194139536097 true] [17592186383384 1041 "selection" 13194139536097 true] [17592186383384 1104 #edb/ref 17592186383387 13194139536097 true] [17592186383384 1110 #uuid "0edb3105-eec6-57bf-a132-e339c8cc4c84" 13194139536097 true] [17592186383385 1034 "{\\begin{aligned}{x}^{′}(t)=-2x(t) \\\\ {y}^{′}(t)=-5y(t)\\end{aligned}" 13194139536097 true] [17592186383385 1103 #edb/ref 17592186045453 13194139536097 true] [17592186383385 1111 #uuid "9ca7fa45-203d-57cc-9e33-c06576540153" 13194139536097 true] [17592186383386 1034 "{\\begin{aligned}{x}^{′}(t)=-5x(t) \\\\ {y}^{′}(t)=-2y(t)\\end{aligned}" 13194139536097 true] [17592186383386 1103 #edb/ref 17592186045453 13194139536097 true] [17592186383386 1111 #uuid "b843cd9a-4d95-5519-9b64-6cd703d2b1f5" 13194139536097 true] [17592186383387 1034 "{\\begin{aligned}{x}^{′}(t)=2x(t) \\\\ {y}^{′}(t)=5y(t)\\end{aligned}" 13194139536097 true] [17592186383387 1103 #edb/ref 17592186045453 13194139536097 true] [17592186383387 1111 #uuid "35878faf-2f32-58fd-8041-eb3a7c9b9dd7" 13194139536097 true] [17592186383388 1034 "{\\begin{aligned}{x}^{′}(t)=-2x(t) \\\\ {y}^{′}(t)=5y(t)\\end{aligned}" 13194139536097 true] [17592186383388 1103 #edb/ref 17592186045453 13194139536097 true] [17592186383388 1111 #uuid "75003487-d53b-5f98-bb5a-3a4e0515ea9f" 13194139536097 true] [17592186383389 1034 "{\\begin{aligned}{x}^{′}(t)=2x(t) \\\\ {y}^{′}(t)=-5y(t)\\end{aligned}" 13194139536097 true] [17592186383389 1103 #edb/ref 17592186045453 13194139536097 true] [17592186383389 1111 #uuid "9a88b7eb-b01e-535d-b28c-b29d8adb3422" 13194139536097 true] [17592186383390 1017 #edb/ref 17592186383391 13194139536097 true] [17592186383390 1089 "q-332710" 13194139536097 true] [17592186383390 1091 #edb/ref 17592186045463 13194139536097 true] [17592186383390 1100 "![](/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/differential/14076662/assets/83a565accc834d7c16091de22eb43ba80c0ce9c4290f567d1e0ccc009beca565.png)\n\nConsider the phase portrait for a decoupled system above.\nWhich of the following systems could correspond to the given portrait?" 13194139536097 true] [17592186383390 1113 #uuid "781b48d3-e667-556c-9dcb-a64c772b6461" 13194139536097 true] [17592186383390 1145 "Writing a decoupled system in matrix form, we have\n\n$$\n{x}^{′}(t)=[\\begin{aligned}{\\lambda }_{1} & 0 \\\\ 0 & {\\lambda }_{2}\\end{aligned}]x(t)\n$$\n\nwhere $x(t)=[\\begin{aligned}x(t) \\\\ y(t)\\end{aligned}].$\n\nSince our system is decoupled, its eigenvectors are parallel to the axes. These correspond to so-called straight-line solutions of the form\n\n$$\n{c}_{1}{e}^{{\\lambda }_{1}t}[\\begin{aligned}1 \\\\ 0\\end{aligned}]\\,\\text{and}\\,{c}_{2}{e}^{{\\lambda }_{2}t}[\\begin{aligned}0 \\\\ 1\\end{aligned}].\n$$\n\nNow, notice that:\n\n- The tangents along the $x$-axis are pointing away from the origin, meaning that the solutions of the form \n${c}_{1}{e}^{{\\lambda }_{1}t}[\\begin{aligned}1 \\\\ 0\\end{aligned}]$\nmust approach infinity as $t\\to \\infty .$ So, we must have ${\\lambda }_{1}>0.$\n\n- The tangents along the $y$-axis are also pointing away from the origin, meaning that the solutions of the form \n${c}_{2}{e}^{{\\lambda }_{2}t}[\\begin{aligned}0 \\\\ 1\\end{aligned}]$\nmust also approach infinity as $t\\to \\infty .$ So, we must have ${\\lambda }_{2}>0.$\n\nAmong the options, the only system that satisfies ${\\lambda }_{1}>0$ and ${\\lambda }_{2}>0$ is the following:\n\n$$\n{\\begin{aligned}{x}^{′}(t)=5x(t) \\\\ {y}^{′}(t)=4y(t)\\end{aligned}\n$$" 13194139536097 true] [17592186383391 1000 #edb/ref 17592186383392 13194139536097 true] [17592186383391 1000 #edb/ref 17592186383393 13194139536097 true] [17592186383391 1000 #edb/ref 17592186383394 13194139536097 true] [17592186383391 1000 #edb/ref 17592186383395 13194139536097 true] [17592186383391 1000 #edb/ref 17592186383396 13194139536097 true] [17592186383391 1040 #edb/ref 17592186045450 13194139536097 true] [17592186383391 1041 "selection" 13194139536097 true] [17592186383391 1104 #edb/ref 17592186383392 13194139536097 true] [17592186383391 1110 #uuid "11e8cfc5-69ae-51bf-bca3-4ed348ffe8c8" 13194139536097 true] [17592186383392 1034 "{\\begin{aligned}{x}^{′}(t)=5x(t) \\\\ {y}^{′}(t)=4y(t)\\end{aligned}" 13194139536097 true] [17592186383392 1103 #edb/ref 17592186045453 13194139536097 true] [17592186383392 1111 #uuid "d546ab91-83e1-5427-8e26-0eb4d4786009" 13194139536097 true] [17592186383393 1034 "{\\begin{aligned}{x}^{′}(t)=5x(t) \\\\ {y}^{′}(t)=-4y(t)\\end{aligned}" 13194139536097 true] [17592186383393 1103 #edb/ref 17592186045453 13194139536097 true] [17592186383393 1111 #uuid "09f62c1c-c516-5303-af46-a437453e5376" 13194139536097 true] [17592186383394 1034 "{\\begin{aligned}{x}^{′}(t)=-5x(t) \\\\ {y}^{′}(t)=4y(t)\\end{aligned}" 13194139536097 true] [17592186383394 1103 #edb/ref 17592186045453 13194139536097 true] [17592186383394 1111 #uuid "5b13e5e4-d888-51fa-8949-3ccdeabdb0ca" 13194139536097 true] [17592186383395 1034 "{\\begin{aligned}{x}^{′}(t)=-5x(t) \\\\ {y}^{′}(t)=-4y(t)\\end{aligned}" 13194139536097 true] [17592186383395 1103 #edb/ref 17592186045453 13194139536097 true] [17592186383395 1111 #uuid "f07067d0-9ecc-5660-a221-4d36dfec149a" 13194139536097 true] [17592186383396 1034 "{\\begin{aligned}{x}^{′}(t)=-4x(t) \\\\ {y}^{′}(t)=-5y(t)\\end{aligned}" 13194139536097 true] [17592186383396 1103 #edb/ref 17592186045453 13194139536097 true] [17592186383396 1111 #uuid "2fd7f1d3-13e4-54f4-a07e-c38ea3bfbb18" 13194139536097 true] [17592186383397 1017 #edb/ref 17592186383398 13194139536097 true] [17592186383397 1089 "q-333193" 13194139536097 true] [17592186383397 1091 #edb/ref 17592186045463 13194139536097 true] [17592186383397 1100 "$$\n{\\begin{aligned}{x}^{′}(t)=-3x(t) \\\\ {y}^{′}(t)=-4y(t)\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nWhich of the following could represent the phase portrait of the decoupled system above?" 13194139536097 true] [17592186383397 1113 #uuid "619ed97d-e84b-53da-afc0-cbcda9b4c479" 13194139536097 true] [17592186383397 1145 "Writing our system in matrix form, we have\n\n$$\n{x}^{′}(t)=[\\begin{aligned}-3 & 0 \\\\ 0 & -4\\end{aligned}]x(t)\n$$\n\nwhere $x(t)=[\\begin{aligned}x(t) \\\\ y(t)\\end{aligned}].$\n\nSince our system is decoupled, its eigenvectors are the following:\n\n- Eigenvalue ${\\lambda }_{1}=-3$ with the corresponding eigenvector $[\\begin{aligned}1 \\\\ 0\\end{aligned}].$\n\n- Eigenvalue ${\\lambda }_{2}=-4$ with the corresponding eigenvector $[\\begin{aligned}0 \\\\ 1\\end{aligned}].$\n\nThe general solution of the system is\n\n$$\nx(t)={c}_{1}{e}^{-3t}[\\begin{aligned}1 \\\\ 0\\end{aligned}]+{c}_{2}{e}^{-4t}[\\begin{aligned}0 \\\\ 1\\end{aligned}].\n$$\n\nNow, notice that:\n\n- The straight-line solutions of the form\n${c}_{1}{e}^{-3t}[\\begin{aligned}1 \\\\ 0\\end{aligned}]$\nlie on the $x$-axis. Since ${e}^{-3t}\\to 0$ as $t\\to \\infty ,$ these solutions approach the origin as $t\\to \\infty .$\n\n- The straight-line solutions of the form\n${c}_{2}{e}^{-4t}[\\begin{aligned}0 \\\\ 1\\end{aligned}]$\nlie on the $y$-axis. Since ${e}^{-4t}\\to 0$ as $t\\to \\infty ,$ these solutions also approach the origin as $t\\to \\infty .$\n\nAmong the given options, there are two phase portraits satisfying these conditions:\n\n![](/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/differential/14076662/assets/3fdbeba46a0f4640e909b0ba489c1aec1fa87a6aa7740f19d08f274b7351d11b.png)\n\nLet's consider the solutions $x(t)={c}_{1}{e}^{-3t}$ and $y(t)={c}_{2}{e}^{-4t}$ provided that ${c}_{1},{c}_{2}\\ne 0.$ Then,\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\frac{\\text{d}y}{\\text{d}x}=\\frac{{y}^{′}(t)}{{x}^{′}(t)}=\\frac{({c}_{2}{e}^{-4t})^{′}}{({c}_{1}{e}^{-3t})^{′}}=\\frac{-4{c}_{2}{e}^{-4t}}{-3{c}_{1}{e}^{-3t}}=\\frac{4{c}_{2}}{3{c}_{1}}{e}^{-t}.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nSo, $\\frac{\\text{d}y}{\\text{d}x}\\to 0$ as $t\\to \\infty ,$ meaning that the tangents to the solution become more and more horizontal as the trajectories approach the origin. This corresponds to the following phase portrait:\n\n![](/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/differential/14076662/assets/4193300af5a8950ce70e2c469812424235665faac279dac0c89289a0307ab46a.png)" 13194139536097 true] [17592186383398 1000 #edb/ref 17592186383399 13194139536097 true] [17592186383398 1000 #edb/ref 17592186383400 13194139536097 true] [17592186383398 1000 #edb/ref 17592186383401 13194139536097 true] [17592186383398 1000 #edb/ref 17592186383402 13194139536097 true] [17592186383398 1000 #edb/ref 17592186383403 13194139536097 true] [17592186383398 1040 #edb/ref 17592186045450 13194139536097 true] [17592186383398 1041 "selection" 13194139536097 true] [17592186383398 1104 #edb/ref 17592186383401 13194139536097 true] [17592186383398 1110 #uuid "2f3b3538-d107-54da-8db2-c4f406514268" 13194139536097 true] [17592186383399 1034 "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/differential/14076662/assets/b414b76f1fe20b5238e75281e57bb851c8e67777e225d7610631dab1b4921b82.png" 13194139536097 true] [17592186383399 1103 #edb/ref 17592186045452 13194139536097 true] [17592186383399 1111 #uuid "b19c52ef-634a-51aa-9a19-9044a1c96217" 13194139536097 true] [17592186383400 1034 "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/differential/14076662/assets/0c70e2c185d054b8cc62411736ecec2e60a716d265819a82a171076d999ef4e6.png" 13194139536097 true] [17592186383400 1103 #edb/ref 17592186045452 13194139536097 true] [17592186383400 1111 #uuid "158c7765-5b2c-57ae-be67-ab360e70b06c" 13194139536097 true] [17592186383401 1034 "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/differential/14076662/assets/e8e9a97ed196b5677e3c2ea6be32f4a1ab04f7721b3d9c41e4db284d69e6805a.png" 13194139536097 true] [17592186383401 1103 #edb/ref 17592186045452 13194139536097 true] [17592186383401 1111 #uuid "07b7a00e-07eb-5c01-a60e-9fce25b101e4" 13194139536097 true] [17592186383402 1034 "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/differential/14076662/assets/d19a6d30c12f5d966578cd950db1f16a9bc309f3a24a8e2480e52edca1281f75.png" 13194139536097 true] [17592186383402 1103 #edb/ref 17592186045452 13194139536097 true] [17592186383402 1111 #uuid "130e6680-2ac5-572b-ab0a-ebbcc4f22140" 13194139536097 true] [17592186383403 1034 "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/diff